.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt SED 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sed
.Nd stream editor (sbase)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nrE
.Ar script
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl nrE
.Fl e Ar script
.Op Fl e Ar script ...
.Op Fl f Ar scriptfile ...
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is sbase 0.1's
.Nm sed ,
built unpatched for ABSD with the C kit; see
.Xr sbase 7 .
It applies an editing script to each line of the named files, or of
standard input, and writes the result to standard output.
.Fl r
and
.Fl E
select extended regular expressions;
.Fl n
prints only what the script prints.
.Sh UNSUPPORTED ON ABSD
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl i
not in sbase 0.1: usage error, status 1.
Write the output to a new file and
.Nm mv
it over the old one.
.It Ic w Ar FILE
writes only where the session may write
.Pq Pa /home/operator , Pa /tmp/session-N ;
elsewhere the open fails with sbase's message.
.It regular expressions
Picolibc's
.Fn regcomp
and
.Fn regexec ;
C locale only (R14), so classes are ASCII.
.It messages
prefixed with
.Ql argv[0]
as invoked;
.Ql strerror
texts are Picolibc's.
.El
.Pp
Behaviour on ABSD is predicted until the guest qualification
.Pq Xr sbase 7 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ sed -n '2,4p' /etc/lines.txt
absd$ sed 's/old/new/g' notes.txt > notes.new
absd$ mv notes.new notes.txt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ed 1 ,
